(2) Similarly, when fulfilling and accumulating the Perfection of Morality (Sīla Pāramī), the Noble Bodhisatta personally experiences the four profits: the Foundation of Truth, which is not transgressing the precepts according to his undertaking; the Foundation of Relinquishment, which is abandoning unwholesome dangers such as the evil intention of immorality; the Foundation of Peace, which is the calming of the danger of misconduct; and the Foundation of Wisdom, which is the possession of great wisdom. Because he experiences these four profits, he continues to develop and fulfill the Perfection of Morality repeatedly and increasingly in the future.

(3) When fulfilling and accumulating the Perfection of Patience (Khantī Pāramī), the Noble Bodhisatta personally experiences the four profits: the Foundation of Truth, which is enduring without fail as he promised; the Foundation of Relinquishment, which is abandoning the proliferation of unwholesome thoughts regarding the wrongs committed or spoken by others; the Foundation of Peace, which is the calming of the outburst of anger (kodha); and the Foundation of Wisdom, which is the possession of great wisdom. Because he experiences these four profits, he continues to develop and fulfill the Perfection of Patience repeatedly and increasingly in the future.

